  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
}

/* Visible focus for keyboard users only; mouse clicks don't show it */
.icon-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid currentColor;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

If the visual size must stay small, 24×24 is the WCAG 2.2 minimum, so use `min-width/min-height: 24px`. Otherwise keep the larger hit area and use padding or spacing to control the layout.

## Notes

- **`role="group"` vs `role="toolbar"`:** I used `group` on purpose. `role="toolbar"` promises arrow-key navigation with a roving tabindex, and you'd have to implement that. With only two buttons, plain Tab navigation is simpler and fine.
- **Icon props:** this assumes `TrashIcon` and `ShareIcon` pass props through to the `<svg>`. If they don't, add `aria-hidden="true"` inside the icon components.
- **Labels:** if the same toolbar appears in a list, make the labels specific (for example `aria-label={`Delete ${item.name}`}`) so users can tell them apart.
- **Tooltips:** sighted mouse users get no hint about what the icons do. Consider adding a `title` or a tooltip. It shouldn't replace `aria-label`.

# Accessibility review

## Issues

1. **Clickable `<div>`s instead of buttons.** They can't be focused with the keyboard, and they don't respond to Enter or Space. Screen readers don't announce them as controls. This is the main problem (WCAG 2.1.1, 4.1.2).
2. **No accessible name.** The controls contain only icons, so a screen reader has nothing to announce (WCAG 4.1.2, 1.1.1).
3. **`outline: none` on `:focus`.** Even if the elements were focusable, keyboard users couldn't see where focus is (WCAG 2.4.7). Because the rule has no replacement style, it removes the only focus indicator.
4. **16×16px hit area.** This is below the WCAG 2.2 minimum target size of 24×24 (2.5.8, AA). 44×44 is the recommended size for touch.
5. **Icons aren't hidden from assistive tech.** Depending on how `TrashIcon` and `ShareIcon` render, an inline SVG can be announced as noise or as a duplicate label.
6. **The toolbar has no semantics.** It's an anonymous `div`, so screen readers don't present the buttons as a related group.

## Fixed component

```tsx
type IconToolbarProps = {
  onDelete: () => void;
  onShare: () => void;
};

export function IconToolbar({ onDelete, onShare }: IconToolbarProps) {
  return (
    <div className="toolbar" role="group" aria-label="Item actions">
      <button type="button" className="icon-btn" onClick={onDelete} aria-label="Delete">
        <TrashIcon aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" />
      </button>
      <button type="button" className="icon-btn" onClick={onShare} aria-label="Share">
        <ShareIcon aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" />
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```css
.icon-btn {
  /* reset native button chrome */
  background: none;
  border: 0;
  padding: 0;
  color: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;

  /* adequate target size; the icon itself can stay 16px */
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-width: 44px;
  min-height: 44px;
  border-radius: 4px;
}

.icon-btn svg {
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
}

/* visible focus for keyboard users, no ring on mouse click */
.icon-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid currentColor; /* or a brand color with ≥3:1 contrast */
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

## Notes

- **Remove the inline `style={{ width: 16, height: 16 }}`** from the buttons. It would shrink the hit area again. Size the SVG instead, as in the CSS above.
- **I used `role="group"` rather than `role="toolbar"`.** The ARIA toolbar pattern requires arrow-key navigation with roving tabindex. Without that, `role="toolbar"` makes a promise the component doesn't keep. With only two buttons, plain Tab navigation is fine. If the toolbar grows, switch to `role="toolbar"` and implement the arrow keys.
- **If your icon components don't forward props,** put `aria-hidden="true"` on the `<svg>` inside them. If they do forward props, the usage above works as written.
- **If the label should be visible,** you can use visible text instead of `aria-label`. If you use a tooltip, keep `aria-label` too.
- **Check contrast.** Icon color against the background needs at least 3:1 (WCAG 1.4.11).
- **Delete is destructive,** so consider a confirmation step or an undo option. That's good UX, though it isn't strictly an accessibility requirement.

